[1mThe one interesting finding.[22m The C reads past the data it writes — [38;5;10mstrcmp[0m on buffers the input never NUL-terminates — so its output depends on the leftover stack contents in [38;5;10mmain[0m's two 1024-byte buffers. A zero-initialized model got ~10% of cases wrong. I recovered the actual startup residue from core dumps of the unmodified reference binary and embedded it ([38;5;10msrc/stack_residue.rs[0m), modeling both buffers as one contiguous region with [38;5;10mref_buffer[0m immediately below [38;5;10minput_buffer[0m, matching the reference frame layout ([38;5;10mrbp-0x830[0m / [38;5;10mrbp-0x430[0m). Independent confirmation: the residue predicts C's stable outputs [38;5;10m-1[0m, [38;5;10m3[0m, and [38;5;10m178[0m for unterminated-input cases without those being fitted to. The residue's observable structure is stable across runs, environment sizes, working directories, and executable path lengths.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two categories remain unmatchable, both genuinely nondeterministic in the C itself:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- Roughly 1 run in 16, a residue byte holding a stack pointer's low byte is zero, which shortens a [38;5;10mstrlen[0m and flips the result. My model reproduces the other 15/16 (confirmed 46/48 on two such inputs).[0m[0m
- The [38;5;10msize_t[0m underflow in [38;5;10mmatch_pattern[0m ([38;5;10mtext_len - pattern_len[0m when the pattern is longer) sends [38;5;10mstrncmp[0m scanning up the stack. When the pattern isn't found, C segfaults — my version segfaults too, with matching exit code 139. When a short pattern [3mis[23m found in the environment area, C prints a different number on every run, so no fixed output can match.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I did not fix any of the C's bugs: the off-by-one length check, the underflow, and the unterminated-buffer reads are all preserved, as is the order of the error checks and the exact [38;5;10mprintf[0m formatting.[0m[0m
